swehouse.c: Sunshine (Makransky) cusps are wrong where w lands exactly on a
quadrant boundary

sunshine_solution_makransky() places each cusp from w, the house circle's
position measured from the meridian. Three of the four boundaries are mishandled
and each fails differently. Together they put 2,848 of 25,632 exactly-landing
cusps in the wrong place; after this patch, none.

    hsys 'i', sundec 0, eps 23.4392911, every integer latitude -89..89
    except 0, armc on each multiple of 30, all twelve cusps, compared with
    the same cusp a millionth of a degree either side:

      discontinuous by more than 1e-4 degrees   before 2848 of 25632
                                                after     0 of 25632

These are not obscure inputs. w is rah -+ q, and with the Sun on the equator q
is 0 exactly, so w is rah and every armc that is a multiple of 30 lands cusps on
a boundary at any latitude. swe_houses_armc() and swe_houses_armc_ex2() take
armc straight from the caller.

1. w exactly 180, both quadrant chains.

Every bound is strict:

    if (w < 90)                  cu = r;
    else if (w > 90 && w < 180)  cu = 180 - r;
    else if (w > 180 && w < 270) cu = 180 + r;
    else                         cu = 360 - r;

so 180 matches none of the first three and falls through to the fourth, which is
meant for the fourth quadrant. r is exactly 0 there because tan(180) is, so the
branch returns 360 where both neighbours return 180.

    #include "swephexp.h"
    #include <stdio.h>
    int main(void) {
      double c[13], a[10]; char s[256];
      a[9] = 20;                    /* Sun declination, required by 'i' */
      swe_houses_armc_ex2(30.0, 0.0, 23.4392911, 'i', c, a, NULL, NULL, s);
      printf("cusp[3] = %.7f", c[3]);   /* 360.0000000, should be 180 */
      return 0;
    }

Neighbouring armc gives 179.9999999 and 180.0000001, and hsys 'I' gives
180.0000000 at the same input: the two Sunshine variants disagree by 180 degrees,
which is what makes this confirmable without a reference implementation.

The second chain, taken when z exceeds 90, has the same strict bound and its own
180 degree error. It is reachable: for houses 5, 6, 8 and 9 the branch is
z = m + ecl, and at w = 180 m is |pole|, so z passes 90 once |pole| does, above
about 74 degrees of latitude. Unpatched, sundec 0, geolat 85, armc 330, cusp 5:

      armc 330 - 1e-7      0.0000001
      armc 330           180.0000000
      armc 330 + 1e-7    359.9999999

Both chains now let 180 belong to the branch above it, where r vanishes and the
two agree.

2. An out-of-range cusp.

With 180 admitted to the z > 90 branch, that branch returns 360 - r with r
exactly 0, so the caller is handed a cusp of exactly 360, outside the [0,360)
every other cusp respects: 50 of them over integer latitude and integer armc.
swe_degnorm() on the way out settles it.

3. w exactly 90 or 270, southern latitudes.

Those two values have branches of their own, with their own formulae and no
180 degree shift, while the general branch ends with one. Which meridian side w
names depends on the hemisphere: a southern chart has had rah turned by 180
already, so the side that frame calls w = 90 is the one a northern chart calls
w = 270. The general branch gets that right through its shift; these two did
not, and a southern chart landing exactly on 90 or 270 came back on the wrong
side of the meridian.

    hsys 'i', geolat -35, sundec 0, eps 23.4392911, cusp 5:

      armc 59.999999   262.071792
      armc 60.000000    97.928207     -> 262.071793
      armc 60.000001   262.071794

Northern charts are untouched by this one: at geolat +35 the same three armc
values give 277.928206, 277.928207 and 277.928208 before and after.

Positions at every other input are unchanged, and no cusp speed moves: over
663,520 random (date, latitude, longitude) configurations spanning 1000 BC to
4100 AD, nothing differs at all. The defect belongs to exact inputs.

Known, not fixed here: the traditional sidereal path uppercases the house
letter (sidereal_houses_trad() takes toupper(hsys) and passes it on), so a
sidereal 'i' silently returns the 'I' solution and this fix never runs there.
Testing 'i' with -sid1 therefore shows no difference and says nothing about
this patch; the fix is reachable tropically and through the armc entry points.

This is a position fix and is independent of the cusp speed patches. It cannot
be combined with "swehouse.c: compute house cusp speeds analytically", which
moves this chain into a function of its own and carries the same correction
there; take one or the other.
---
 swehouse.c | 31 +++++++++++++++++------------
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)
diff --git a/swehouse.c b/swehouse.c
index 1c5bbd3..82230f0 100644
--- a/swehouse.c
+++ b/swehouse.c
@@ -2982,18 +2982,21 @@ static int sunshine_solution_makransky(double ramc, double lat, double ecl, stru
       w = swe_degnorm(rah - q);
     else
       w = swe_degnorm(rah + q);
-    if (w == 90) {
-      r = atand(sind(ecl) * tand(pole));
-      if (ih <= 3 || ih >= 11)
-        cu = 90 + r;
-      else
-        cu = 90 - r;
-    } else if (w == 270) {
+    if (w == 90 || w == 270) {
+      /* Which meridian side w names depends on the hemisphere. Southern charts
+       * have already had rah turned by 180 above, so the side this frame calls
+       * w == 90 is the one a northern chart calls w == 270. The general branch
+       * below gets that right by way of its own 180 shift; these two had their
+       * own formulae and no shift, so a southern chart landing exactly on 90 or
+       * 270 came back on the wrong side of the meridian. */
+      int at90 = (w == 90);
+      if (lat < 0) at90 = !at90;
       r = atand(sinecl * tand(pole));
-      if (ih <= 3 || ih >= 11)
-        cu = 270 - r;
-      else
-        cu = 270 + r;
+      if (at90) {
+        if (ih <= 3 || ih >= 11) cu = 90 + r; else cu = 90 - r;
+      } else {
+        if (ih <= 3 || ih >= 11) cu = 270 - r; else cu = 270 + r;
+      }
     } else {
       double m;
       m = atand(fabs(tand(pole) / cosd(w)));
@@ -3020,7 +3023,7 @@ static int sunshine_solution_makransky(double ramc, double lat, double ecl, stru
 	  cu = r;
 	else if (w > 90 && w < 180)
 	  cu = 180 - r;
-	else if (w > 180 && w < 270)
+	else if (w >= 180 && w < 270)
 	  cu = 180 + r;
 	else
 	  cu = 360 - r;
@@ -3032,7 +3035,7 @@ static int sunshine_solution_makransky(double ramc, double lat, double ecl, stru
 	  cu = 180 - r;
 	else if (w > 90 && w < 180)
 	  cu = + r;
-	else if (w > 180 && w < 270)
+	else if (w >= 180 && w < 270)
 	  cu = 360 - r;
 	else
 	  cu = 180 + r;
@@ -3040,7 +3043,7 @@ static int sunshine_solution_makransky(double ramc, double lat, double ecl, stru
       if (lat < 0)	// Makransky deals with southern latitude this way
         cu = swe_degnorm(cu + 180);
     }
-    hsp->cusp[ih] = cu;
+    hsp->cusp[ih] = swe_degnorm(cu);
   } 
   return OK;
 }
